{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261118T183935Z-615e9065", "ts": "2026-11-18T18:42:51Z", "actor": {"worktree": ".claude/worktrees/server-image", "plugin": "3.8.2-dev.1", "branch": "fix/server-image-test-target", "build": "3682719168c6"}, "body": {"base_commit": "857e608c24f67589be4818b97fd60af603329523", "base_tree": "4bebf978b038b87d56bb134f108d802bc5255c01", "head_tree": "b8cff47dd68f978a2519c59e67749805de3ad3b7", "head_commit": "d2c5d16675d58f47b008864c6ee8b22e654846c6", "dispatch_commit": "d2c5d16675d58f47b008864c6ee8b22e654846c6", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": [".github/workflows/suites.yml", "arrt/Dockerfile", "arrt/tests/unit/test_wall_state_words.py", "deploy/README.md"], "files_changed": [".github/workflows/suites.yml", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-server-image.md", ".prawduct/changes/server-image.md", "arrt/Dockerfile", "arrt/tests/unit/test_wall_state_words.py", "deploy/README.md"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-server-image.md", ".prawduct/changes/server-image.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "The devcontainer image's stated reason for existing still says arrt/Dockerfile's test stage runs as root", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. It broke because the fix (USER 568:568 in the test stage) changed the subject of a justification that sits outside the diff. `.devcontainer/Dockerfile` lines 3-5 explain why testament needs its own image: \"`arrt/Dockerfile` builds with `arrt/` as its context and its test stage runs as root, and testament builds every image from the repo root and refuses one that ends as root (#386).\" After this change the test stage ends as uid 568, and this branch closes #386, so half of that reason is now false. The other half (the `arrt/` context against testament's repo-root build) still holds. Anyone deciding whether the two images can be merged, or whether testament can build the test target directly, is given a blocker that no longer exists. Fix: delete the root clause and keep the reason that still holds (the build context, and that the devcontainer carries node and git, which the deploy image does not). Don't reword it into history. This is the shape of the core learning \"When you retire, amend, descope or close anything, first grep the whole repo (no --include) for its nouns and OLD wording, justifying prose and 'there is no X' included\": `git grep -i 'as root'` finds it.", "files": ["arrt/Dockerfile", ".devcontainer/Dockerfile"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "rule-unenforced: the image leg runs a suite with no skip guard, the per-job case test_assert_tests_ran.py hands to the Critic", "recommendation": "The rule is written down. docs/testing.md says a skip in CI 'is a trap... `.github/scripts/assert_tests_ran.py` is what closes that', and every other suite leg in suites.yml and browser.yml runs that guard. The only check, `tests/test_assert_tests_ran.py::test_every_workflow_that_runs_a_suite_calls_the_guard`, matches per workflow file. Its docstring leaves this exact case out on purpose: 'a second pytest job added to a workflow that already calls the guard elsewhere would pass unseen... The Critic reviews new jobs'. The new `image` job is that case. Its pytest also runs inside a Dockerfile `RUN`, so `_ci_pytest_invocations` cannot see it either. In the image, every `tests/browser` module skips via `importorskip` (the browser group is not installed, and the run has no `--ignore=tests/browser`), and the node-gated modules (test_ages, test_route_parsing, test_client_vocabulary, test_one_date_formatter) skip because node is absent. So the leg already passes with dozens of skips. A future layout or install break in the image that shows up as a skip (the kind of difference this job exists to catch, per its own comment) builds green, and no one is told. Per the core learning 'When a check can be quiet for more than one reason, give each quiet state its own output', a skip from missing provisioning and an accepted skip need different outputs. Instance fix: scope the image run like the curation leg (`pytest tests --ignore=tests/browser --junitxml=...`), decide whether node is provisioned in the test stage or the node-gated skips are accepted, get the report out of the build (e.g. an export stage, or run the guard in the Dockerfile after pytest) and feed it to `assert_tests_ran.py`. If skips are accepted, record that and why in the job comment. Class fix (mechanizing the rule): make the workflow guard test per-job rather than per-file, and count a `RUN ... pytest` in a Dockerfile that a workflow builds as an invocation. Bound the search to `.github/workflows/*.y*ml` and `arrt/Dockerfile`.", "files": [".github/workflows/suites.yml", "arrt/Dockerfile"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "blocking", "title": "record-lint chunk-ref-missing: chunk 01:47", "recommendation": "declared deliverable `tools/` file does not exist", "source": "record-lint", "fid": "R-3"}], "counts": {"blocking": 1, "warning": 2, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "The image leg is the one CI test leg with no 'nothing actually ran' guard, so a skip caused by the image passes silently", "recommendation": "The root, curation and contract legs each pipe their JUnit report through `.github/scripts/assert_tests_ran.py`. The new `image` job runs `pytest -q` inside `docker build`, so an empty collection still fails it (exit 5), but a skip does not. The image already skips some tests on purpose (`tests/browser` importorskips its group; `test_ages.py`, `test_route_parsing.py`, `test_client_vocabulary.py` and `test_one_date_formatter.py` skip without `node`). A test that starts skipping because of how the image is laid out, which is the class this job exists to catch, would show green. It is an observation because today's skips are all deliberate and documented. If it is ever wanted, the cheapest mechanism is a pinned allow-list of the image's expected skip reasons, checked from the build output. Learnings: 'When a check can be quiet for more than one reason, give each quiet state its own output'. Instance.", "files": [".github/workflows/suites.yml", "arrt/Dockerfile"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "The plan carries no governed_by: field, though the product has Direction sections", "recommendation": "`build-plan-server-image.md` lists only `depends_on: architecture`. Four sibling plans record `governed_by:`, and `architecture.md`, `data-model.md`, `design-direction.md`, `nonfunctional-requirements.md` and `accessibility-spec.md` all carry `## Direction`. I read the diff (a CI job, the test stage of `arrt/Dockerfile`, one test's path, one README sentence) and found no departure from any Direction norm or `project-preferences.md` row. In particular the app stage is unchanged, and the one-uv / one-Python row still holds because the job uses the Dockerfile's pinned uv. So there is no disposition here to check, and the only gap is the field. Seeding it with `prawduct-hook jurisdiction` would make that a recorded check instead of a reviewer's reading.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-server-image.md"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "The image's green run is asserted by the plan's Done-when but not recorded", "recommendation": "The recorded test evidence (tree b8cff47d, 6469 passed, 0 failed) is the host run of all suites. It does not include the in-image run. The change record gives the red count on the unfixed tree (4 failed, 3 errors) but names no green run of the `image` job on the fixed tree. That green run is the only evidence for the plan's first goal, 'The test target passes in the image as built for the NAS'. The PR's own CI run of the job will supply it. No edit is wanted unless that run is red.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/server-image.md"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The image job has no guard against skips, unlike every sibling suite job", "recommendation": "Every other test job in `suites.yml` runs `assert_tests_ran.py`, which treats a skip as a provisioning failure. The new `image` job relies on the Docker RUN's exit status, which fails on red or on nothing collected but passes when tests skip. Today the image skips the node-gated client checks (test_ages, test_client_vocabulary, test_one_date_formatter, test_route_parsing) and the browser modules. None of those is about the image, so nothing image-relevant is hidden yet. But the job exists to catch an image that 'installs ... something differently', and a missing system dependency that shows up as an importorskip or skipif would turn green here. If that class matters, write a JUnit report in the RUN and check it against an explicit allowlist of skips expected in the image. Otherwise no change is wanted.", "files": [".github/workflows/suites.yml", "arrt/Dockerfile"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The app user's uid is written twice in the Dockerfile", "recommendation": "The test stage's `USER 568:568` is described as 'the app stage's user', but it is a second copy of the app stage's literal. If the app stage's uid changes, the test stage keeps vouching for the old one and nothing notices. A global `ARG APP_UID=568` referenced by both stages would keep them as one value. This is cheap and worth folding into a commit that is already happening; otherwise accept it.", "files": ["arrt/Dockerfile"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "`--no-sync` on the test stage's pytest line carries no reason", "recommendation": "Changing `USER` to 568 makes a syncing `uv run` fail: /opt/venv is root-owned, and 'It owns nothing in the image' hints at this. A maintainer who drops `--no-sync` while tidying will get a permission error at build time, which is loud, so no edit is needed. If you are touching the line anyway, put the constraint in its comment.", "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Records Pass: covered .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-server-image.md and .prawduct/changes/server-image.md; neither clears a bar", "recommendation": "No edit wanted. I checked the plan's two DECISION entries (where the job lives, amd64 on an amd64 runner) against the diff, and they match it. I also checked the change record's claims: tools/ is copied, the stage runs as the app user 568, walls.js is read from STATIC_DIR, and the app stage is unchanged (the Consumer note 'none' holds). All are true of the Dockerfile, the test and the workflow. The '4 failed and 3 errors' figure is an inert count that nothing reads. The `chunk-ref-missing` lint result on chunk 01 belongs to record-lint and is not restated here.", "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "record-lint chunk-ref-missing: unchecked", "recommendation": "chunk-ref-missing graded chunk 01 of .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-server-image.md: chunk 01 was inferred as the plan's first open chunk (`plan_status.current_chunks`) because the dispatch carried no chunk \u2014 a chunk whose review is not yet recorded is still open, so this may be the next chunk rather than the reviewed one", "source": "record-lint", "oid": "O-8"}], "duration_seconds": 420, "scope": "server-image", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": [], "chunks_proposed": [], "base_reviewed": "857e608c24f67589be4818b97fd60af603329523", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-server-image.md", ".prawduct/changes/server-image.md", "deploy/README.md"], "chunk_graded": "